    "text": "\n\nHere's an example of a familiar weather pattern, El Niño:\n\nEl Niño is a natural phenomenon that occurs every two to seven years in the Pacific Ocean. It's caused when the surface waters of the Pacific Ocean become warmer than normal, particularly off the coast of South America.\n\nThis warming disrupts the trade winds that normally push surface waters westward across the Pacific. As a result, the warm water moves eastward, affecting weather patterns globally.\n\nThe warmer waters cause changes in atmospheric pressure, which can lead to heavy rains in some regions like California",
